      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": "<span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /> <u style=\"font-size: 21px; font-weight: 700; display: inline !important;\">Shortcut Trick</u></span>\n<p>In a <strong>rhombus</strong>, the diagonals <strong>bisect</strong> the interior angles of the vertices.</p>\n<p>Given ∠CDO = 50°, the total angle at vertex D is ∠ADC = 2 × ∠CDO = 2 × 50° = 100°.</p>\n<p>In a rhombus, <strong>adjacent angles</strong> are supplementary (sum = 180°).</p>\n<p>∠DAB + ∠ADC = 180°</p>\n<p>∠DAB = 180° − 100° = 80°.</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 80°.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Alternate Method</u></span></strong></span></p>\n<p><strong>Given:</strong> ∠CDO = 50° in <strong>Rhombus</strong> ABCD where <strong>diagonals</strong> AC and BD intersect at O.</p>\n<p><strong>Property Used:</strong> Diagonals of a rhombus are <strong>perpendicular</strong> to each other and <strong>bisect</strong> the internal angles.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: left;\"><img src=\"https://cdn.testbook.com/tb_solImg_8d1eda60.png/1784029863.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"></div>\n<p>⇒ In ΔCDO, ∠COD = 90° (Diagonals are perpendicular)</p>\n<p>⇒ ∠CDO = 50° (Given)</p>\n<p>⇒ In ΔCDO: ∠DCO = 180° − (90° + 50°) = 40°</p>\n<p>⇒ Since diagonal AC <strong>bisects</strong> ∠BCD, ∠BCD = 2 × ∠DCO</p>\n<p>⇒ ∠BCD = 2 × 40° = 80°</p>\n<p>⇒ In a rhombus, <strong>opposite angles</strong> are equal: ∠DAB = ∠BCD</p>\n<p>⇒ ∠DAB = 80°</p>\n<p><strong>∴ The correct answer is 80°.</strong></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"display: flex;gap: 6px;align-items: center;\"><img height=\"26px\" src=\"//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png\" width=\"26px\" /><strong><span style=\"vertical-align: middle;font-size: 21px;\"><u>Additional Information</u></span></strong></span></p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;\">Properties of a Rhombus</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;\">All four sides are equal, and opposite sides are parallel. Diagonals bisect each other at 90°.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;\">Angle Bisector Property</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;\">Each diagonal of a rhombus bisects the interior angles of the vertices it joins.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;\">Area of a Rhombus</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;\">The area can be calculated using the diagonals: Area = 1/2 × d<sub>1</sub> × d<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:8px 0 2px 0;font-weight:700;color:#222222;\">Consecutive Angles</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 8px 0;color:#444444;\">Any two adjacent (consecutive) angles in a rhombus are supplementary, meaning they sum to 180°.</p>",
      "images": [
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/shortcut-trick-image.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/alternate-methord-image.png",
        "https://cdn.testbook.com/tb_solImg_8d1eda60.png/1784029863.png",
        "//cdn.testbook.com/resources/lms_creative_elements/additional-information-image.png"
      ],
