    {
      "id": "77068cbebed6-q10",
      "number": 10,
      "question": "The figure formed by joining the consecutive midpoints of the sides of a quadrilateral ABCD is a rhombus if and only if ABCD is a ____.",
      "questionHtml": "The figure formed by joining the consecutive midpoints of the sides of a quadrilateral ABCD is a rhombus if and only if ABCD is a ____.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Rhombus",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">Rhombus</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Rectangle",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">Rectangle</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Trapezium",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">Trapezium</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "None of the above",
          "html": "<span style=\"\">None of the above</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "solution": "<p style=\"\"><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n\n<p>The formed&nbsp;figure is a Rhombus</p>\n\n<p><strong>Concept Used:&nbsp;</strong></p>\n\n<p>1. The midpoint theorem states that &ldquo;The line segment in a triangle joining the midpoint of any two sides of the triangle is said to be parallel to its third side and is also half of the length of the third side.&rdquo;</p>\n\n<p>2. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Let M, N, P, and Q be the midpoints of the sides AB, BC, CD, and AD of the quadrilateral ABCD, respectively.<br />\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/23/10/F1_SSC_Arbaz_4-10-23_D1_v2.png\" style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41); height: 156px; width: 191px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>In triangles ABD and BAC:</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; Since M and N are midpoints, by the midpoint theorem,</p>\n\n<p>MN must be parallel to AC, and MN = 1/2 AC.</p>\n\n<p>In triangles ABC and ADC:</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; Since N and P are midpoints, by the midpoint theorem,</p>\n\n<p>NP must be parallel to BD, and NP = 1/2 BD.</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span>MN is parallel to AC and NP is parallel to BD</p>\n\n<p>But one major point to notice is that both these pairs of opposite sides (MN and NP) are equal being half the diagonals AC and AD respectively.</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span>A quadrilateral having opposite sides equal as well as parallel is a Rhombus.</p>\n\n<p>So, MNPQ is a Rhombus.</p>\n\n<p>However, for MNPQ to be a Rhombus only through joining the midpoints of each of the sides of a Quadrilateral,</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"color: rgb(33, 37, 41);\">&rArr;&nbsp;</span>The original quadrilateral ABCD must be Rectangle.</p>\n\n<p>This is due to the fact that in a Rectangle,</p>\n\n<p>&rArr; The diagonals are equal (AC = BD).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Therefore, if a rhombus is formed by joining the midpoints of a quadrilateral, the original quadrilateral is a rectangle.</strong></p>\n<span style=\"display: none;\">&nbsp;</span>",
